  "notes": "Source table on page 63 (Table C-5) provides active member counts by age (columns) and service (rows). Target rows are age buckets, mapped by summing source rows where necessary (e.g., service rows 0-4 sum to target age <25). Target columns are service buckets, mapped by summing source columns where necessary (e.g., source age columns <20 and 20-24 sum to target service 4). The target row '70' (age 65+) has no corresponding source column as the source table's age columns stop at '70+' which is the last column, but the target row '70' corresponds to age 65+. Wait, the target rows are Age buckets and columns are Service buckets. Source table has Age as columns and Service as rows. Target rows are Age, Target cols are Service. So I need to map Source Age columns to Target Age rows, and Source Service rows to Target Service columns. Source Age columns: <20, 20-24, 25-29, 30-34, 35-39, 40-44, 45-49, 50-54, 55-59, 60-64, 65-69, 70+. Target Age rows: <25, 29, 34, 39, 44, 49, 54, 59, 64, 70. Mapping: <25 = sum(<20, 20-24). 29 = 25-29. 34 = 30-34. 39 = 35-39. 44 = 40-44. 49 = 45-49. 54 = 50-54. 59 = 55-59. 64 = 60-64. 70 = 65-69 + 70+ (sum). Source Service rows: 0, 1, 2, 3-4, 5-9, 10-14, 15-19, 20-24, 25-29, 30-34, 35-39, 40+. Target Service cols: 4, 9, 14, 19, 24, 29, 34, 40. Mapping: 4 = sum(0, 1, 2, 3-4). 9 = 5-9. 14 = 10-14. 19 = 15-19. 24 = 20-24. 29 = 25-29. 34 = 30-34. 40 = sum(35-39, 40+). Corrected row_map and col_map in the JSON above to reflect this logic.",
